  "problem": "A factory produces two types of sugar: brown and extra-refined. Every year, the factory's sales executives study how customers' preferences change between the two types of sugar. The table below shows the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Brown** | **Extra-Refined** |\n| To: | **Brown** | $0.8$ | $1$ |\n| **Extra-Refined** | $0.2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nIt's found that $20%$ of customers who currently buy brown sugar will switch to extra-refined sugar next year, while $100%$ of customers who currently purchase extra-refined sugar will switch to brown sugar next year.\n\nAssuming that there are $450\\,000$ customers in total and that the given probabilities remain constant over many years, how many customers will eventually consume extra-refined sugar?",
